<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaykhatu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1295">1295</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaykhatu was the Mongol ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1291 to 1295, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a>. His extravagance emptied the treasury, and in 1294 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> modelled on the Chinese chao, which brought trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> to a halt and was quickly withdrawn. He was overthrown and killed in 1295 by his cousin Baydu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaykhatu.jpg" alt="Gaykhatu" width="304" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shams al-Dîn Kâshânî · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GeikhatuEnthroned_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
